# Tickets, questions and pull requests

Read this before working on a {{ tracker_name }} ticket, asking a question about a ticket or opening a pull request, in the meta-repository or in a clone. The branch flow itself is in [branches.md](branches.md).

## {{ tracker_name }} is read-only

The agent may read {{ tracker_name }} tickets, their comments, their status and their attached files.

The agent changes nothing in {{ tracker_name }}: it does not create or edit tickets, comment, change a status, upload files or log work. The agent does not change a ticket's status after opening a pull request either; the developer moves the ticket. The tracker belongs to the team and its clients, and an agent's write there cannot be taken back quietly. This rule is written only; no hook checks it.

When {{ tracker_name }} cannot be reached or an attached file cannot be read, the agent names the ticket or attachment it could not read and asks the developer to paste its text or export it to a readable format. It does not guess the content.

## Attachments

A file the agent downloads from a ticket goes to `openspec/changes/<change>/sources/` when the ticket has an OpenSpec change, so the change keeps its sources. When the ticket has no OpenSpec change, the file goes to a temporary folder outside every repository: not in the meta-repository and not in a clone, so it never ends up in a commit.

## Questions about a ticket

The agent does not post questions to {{ tracker_name }}. It gives them in the chat, and the developer posts them as a ticket comment.

- The questions use the ticket's language.
- They are a numbered list, one short question per line.
- Nothing comes before or after the list: no greeting, no introduction, no closing.

The developer copies the list as it is, so anything else in it is extra work.

### In an OpenSpec change

- The structured clarifying questions keep their own format.
- For a question only the ticket's author can answer, the agent also gives the short paste-ready version.
- When the developer brings the reply to the chat, the agent records it as the answer in the change's `clarifications.md`.

{% else -%}
# Tasks and pull requests

Read this before starting a task or opening a pull request, in the meta-repository or in a clone. The branch flow itself is in [branches.md](branches.md).

## Questions about a task

Unclear points of a task go to the developer in the chat as a numbered list, one short question per line, with nothing before or after it, so the developer can forward the list as it is. In an OpenSpec change the structured clarifying questions keep their own format.

{% endif -%}
## Before each task

Before each new ticket or task, and before creating its branch, the agent runs `mise run pull` and then `mise run status`. `mise run pull` updates the meta-repository and every clone, so the work starts from the latest code and rules.

- When `mise run pull` lists a repository under "Not updated, resolve manually", the agent names that repository and the reason (missing clone, failed pull, conflict, leftover stash) and does not start the task until the developer resolves it.
- A repository listed under "No upstream, not updated" does not stop the task. It is usually an unpushed work branch. The agent runs `git -C <repository> fetch origin` and creates the new work branch from the freshly fetched `origin/main` or `origin/dev`, as the repository's branch model requires ([branches.md](branches.md)).

## Pull requests

### When the agent opens one

For work with an OpenSpec change{% if tracker != 'none' %} and a ticket{% endif %}, the agent opens the pull request without asking once all three conditions hold:

1. Every task of the change is done.
2. The repository's tests and quality checks pass.
3. Every point of the {% if tracker != 'none' %}ticket and of the answers to its questions{% else %}change and of the answers to its questions{% endif %} is covered.

The target is the rest branch in a clone and `main` in the meta-repository. The developer reviews the pull request and merges it by hand; the agent never merges ([branches.md](branches.md)).

For work without an OpenSpec change{% if tracker != 'none' %} or without a ticket{% endif %}, the agent opens a pull request only when the developer asks for it.

The automatic pull request applies only when agent commit and push are both allowed (`METAREPO_AGENT_COMMIT` and `METAREPO_AGENT_PUSH`, [agent-settings.md](agent-settings.md)). Otherwise the agent does not open the pull request: it says the work is ready and reports the branch, what is left to do (commit, push, pull request) and a ready pull request title and description.

When a pull request from the same branch into the same target is already open, the agent does not open a second one and gives the link to the open one. New commits pushed to the branch update it.

### Title and description

Every pull request the agent opens has the same form in every repository.
{%- if tracker == 'github' %}

- Title: a Conventional Commits title such as `fix: correct the invoice total`.
- Description: what changed, how it was tested, and `Closes #123` for the issue it resolves (`Closes owner/repo#123` for an issue in another repository).
- Work without an issue: the same title, with no `Closes` line.
{%- elif tracker != 'none' %}

- Title: `{{ 'ENG' if tracker == 'linear' else 'PROJ' }}-123: short summary`, that is `<ticket key>: <short summary>`.
- Description: what changed, how it was tested, and the link to the ticket.
- Work without a ticket: a Conventional Commits title such as `docs: update agent rules`, with no ticket key and no ticket link.
{%- else %}

- Title: a Conventional Commits title such as `docs: update agent rules`.
- Description: what changed and how it was tested.
{%- endif %}
- Pull request and commit texts are in {{ language_name }} and carry no Claude attribution ([branches.md](branches.md)).

### Main-based clone: pull request into main

The agent opens the pull request of a main-based work branch into `main` only after the developer says the change is approved by QA. It comes from the work branch, never from `dev` or a merge branch. The agent does not watch QA on its own.
{% endif -%}
